{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 17, "model_name": "mistralai/mixtral-8x7b-instruct-v0.1", "content": " Raymond III Count of Tripoli\n1st Count of Tripoli from House of Poitiers\nBorn 1140 Died 1187\nFather Raymond II Count of Tripoli Mother Hodierna of Jerusalem\nConsort Eschiva of Saint Omer\nChildren Bohemond IV of Antioch Raymond IV of Tripoli\nReign 1152-1187 Predecessor Raymond II of Tripoli Successor Bohemond III of Antioch\n\n*Early life and accession*\nBorn in 1140 Raymond III was the only son of Raymond II Count of Tripoli and Hodierna of Jerusalem. At the age of 12 he succeeded his father as Count of Tripoli in 1152.\n\n*Reign*\nRaymond III faced numerous challenges during his reign. He was involved in a power struggle with the counts of Flanders and Hainaut over the regency of the Kingdom of Jerusalem. In 1153, Raymond III was captured by Nur ad-Din, the Zengid ruler of Aleppo and Damascus, but was released the following year.\n\nIn 1156, Raymond III was again captured by Nur ad-Din, this time during the Battle of Harim. He was released in 1158 in exchange for the release of several Muslim prisoners.\n\nRaymond III played a prominent role in the defense of the Kingdom of Jerusalem during the Second Crusade. He also participated in the Battle of Ascalon in 1153 and the Siege of Shaizar in 1157.\n\nIn 1164, Raymond III was captured for a third time, this time by Nur ad-Din's successor, Saladin. He was released in 1173.\n\nRaymond III's reign was marked by a series of conflicts with the Knights Templar and the Knights Hospitaller. He also had a tumultuous relationship with his cousin, King Amalric I of Jerusalem.\n\n*Later life and death*\nIn 1174, Raymond III supported the election of Baldwin IV of Jerusalem, who was a leper and a minor. Raymond III served as regent until Baldwin IV came of age.\n\nIn 1183, Raymond III supported the election of Sibylla of Jerusalem, the sister of Baldwin IV, as queen. He also supported the marriage 